<description>
Composite three stacked same-size panels into one panel, with the bottom panel painted last.

Input structure
- The input is three equal-height panels stacked vertically on a common background.
- Each panel contains exactly one foreground color.
- Corresponding cells across the three panels occupy the same output position.

Rule
- Start with the middle panel as the base.
- Copy every non-background pixel from the top panel onto the same position.
- Copy every non-background pixel from the bottom panel onto the same position, overwriting both earlier layers.

Key insight
- Cell conflicts are resolved by panel position/layer role: middle first, then top, then bottom, not by numeric color order.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "three equal-size single-color foreground masks stacked vertically on a black background",
  "transform": "compose corresponding cells into one panel: middle panel is the base, top panel overwrites non-background cells, and bottom panel overwrites both",
  "grid_shape": "same height and width as one stacked panel"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 15x5 (three 5x5 panels stacked vertically) [observed]
  background: 0 (black)
  panels:
    layout: top / middle / bottom, each panel_h x W
    panel_h: 3-10, W: 3-10
    colors: each panel has exactly 1 foreground color; all 3 colors are distinct non-bg
    color_assignment: top=1, mid=8, bot=6 [observed]; solver accepts any 3 distinct
    fill_ratio: 36%-72% of panel cells are foreground per panel
    connectivity: 1-8 connected components per panel (no constraint on contiguity)
  overlap:
    every pair of panels must share >=1 foreground cell position (ensures compositing is non-trivial)
    all 3 panel colors must be visible in output (not fully occluded)
  transform:
    output = composite of 3 panels at same (r,c) positions
    layering order (back to front): middle, top, bottom
    bottom wins all conflicts; top wins over middle; middle fills remaining

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick panel_h in 3-10, W in 3-10, and 3 distinct non-bg colors (c_top, c_mid, c_bot)
    2. Build output panel (panel_h x W) by randomly assigning each cell to one of {bg, c_top, c_mid, c_bot}, with fill ~85-96% non-bg and each color covering >=1 cell
    3. Derive the 3 input panels from the output. For each cell (r,c):
       - if output=c_bot -> bot panel gets c_bot; top/mid may or may not have foreground (random)
       - if output=c_top -> top panel gets c_top, bot panel must be bg; mid may or may not have foreground
       - if output=c_mid -> mid panel gets c_mid, top and bot must be bg
       - if output=bg -> all 3 panels are bg at (r,c)
    4. Verify each panel has fill ratio 36-72% and each pair overlaps >=1 cell; retry if not
    5. Stack top/mid/bot vertically to form the 3*panel_h x W input grid
```
